# Architecture

Each layer knows only the layer below it:

`View (SwiftUI)` → `ViewModel` → `Repository` → `Service (TMDB, persistence)`

## Folders

The source tree follows that arrow. A screen may use domain types and an injected repository. It does not own DTOs, request building, or a disk store.

- `App/` — composition root, plus `AppDelegate` and `SceneDelegate`.
- `Screens/<Feature>/` — the view, its view model, and subviews that exist for that screen.
- `Screens/Shared/` — empty, error, and other chrome reused across screens.
- `Design/` — tokens and visual primitives.
- `Navigation/` — tabs, routes, and routers.
- `Data/Domain/` — models the rest of the app is allowed to see.
- `Data/Repositories/` — mapping, paging, and the API screens call.
- `Data/Networking/` — the HTTP client, TMDB requests, and DTOs.
- `Data/Persistence/` — disk stores.
- `Data/Resources/` — bundled data, including the awards catalog.
- `Support/` — `AppError`, `LoadState`, logging, and `ImageLoader`.

Tests use the same split under `TheSilverScreenTests/` (`Screens`, `Data`, `Navigation`, `Support`).

## MVVM

- One view model per screen: `@Observable @MainActor final class MovieListViewModel`.
- View models import `Foundation` only — never `SwiftUI` or `UIKit`.
- Expose one explicit state enum. No parallel `isLoading` / `errorMessage` / `movies` flags that can disagree.

```swift
enum LoadActivity: Sendable, Equatable {
    case none, refreshing, loadingMore
    case failed(AppError)      // a refresh failed, but the content on screen is still good
}

enum LoadState<Value: Sendable>: Sendable {
    case idle                                   // nothing requested yet
    case loading                                // first load, nothing to show
    case empty                                  // succeeded, no results
    case loaded(Value, activity: LoadActivity)  // content on screen
    case failed(AppError)                       // first load failed, nothing to show

    static func loaded(_ value: Value) -> Self { .loaded(value, activity: .none) }
}

extension LoadState: Equatable where Value: Equatable {}
```

Two things this shape buys, both of which a four-case `idle / loading / loaded / failed` enum gets wrong:

- **Refresh, paging, and refresh-failure keep the content on screen.** Pull-to-refresh is `.loaded(movies, activity: .refreshing)`, not `.loading` — going back to `.loading` throws the list away and flashes a spinner. A failed refresh is `.loaded(movies, activity: .failed(.offline))`, so the user keeps twenty good movies and gets a toast, instead of a full-screen error replacing content that was fine. Only a **cold** failure with nothing to show is `.failed`.
- **`empty` is its own case.** Do not write `case .loaded(let movies) where movies.isEmpty` at each call site; one screen will forget the `where` clause and ship a blank list.

`LoadActivity` is nested inside `.loaded` rather than sitting beside it, so "loading" and "has content" cannot disagree — which is the same reason we banned parallel `isLoading` flags. `Equatable` is conditional so tests can assert `viewModel.state == .failed(.offline)` without forcing every payload to be `Equatable`.

- Views render state and forward intent (`await viewModel.load()`). No networking, mapping, or persistence in a view.

## Repositories

- Screens depend on a repository, never on a networking type.
- A repository owns its API service, mapping, caching, and persistence, and returns domain models or throws `AppError`.
- The persistence store stays private to the repository.

## Dependency injection — no singletons

No `static let shared`, no global mutable state, no dependency constructed inside the type that uses it. Build dependencies once in the app entry point (the composition root) and pass them down: init injection for view models, `Environment` for views.

```swift
// ❌ BAD
final class MovieListViewModel {
    private let controller = MovieController()
}

// ✅ GOOD
final class MovieListViewModel {
    private let movies: MovieRepository
    init(movies: MovieRepository) { self.movies = movies }
}
```

## Navigation

- `AppRouter` owns `selectedTab` plus one `NavigationRouter` per tab; each `NavigationRouter` owns its own `path: [Route]`.
- Views push routes (`router.push(.movieDetail(id))`). They never construct a destination view.
- A cross-tab deep link sets `selectedTab`, then pushes onto that tab's router.

## SOLID without protocol sprawl

- Add a protocol only when a second real implementation exists or a test must substitute an external boundary (network, disk, clock).
- No protocol per type, and no protocol wrapping a single concrete struct.
- Prefer several small types with one reason to change over one type with mode flags.

Concretely, this app has three protocols and no others: `HTTPClient` (network), the Library lists store (disk), and `AppLogging`. **Repositories and view models are concrete types.** There is no `MovieRepositoryProtocol`, and tests do not fake a repository — see [`testing.mdc`](.cursor/rules/testing.mdc).

## Target toolchain

Swift 6 language mode with strict concurrency, Observation, and an iOS 26 deployment target.

Screens are SwiftUI. `AppDelegate`, `SceneDelegate`, and `ImageLoader`'s `UIImage` cache are the only UIKit that stays.
